{
  "name": "vector_light_surface_lift",
  "saveFile": "minimal_colony.rws",
  "description": "The surface lift: the beam brightens the surface it lands on instead of adding light beside it. Reported from play as \"the vector lighting through a door is a lot brighter than outdoors and notably, doesn't actually light the other room up - no features are lit, just the additional glow\", and both halves are one property of the shader max's compositing rather than of its level: an additive pass above the lighting overlay's multiply adds the same amount of light to a pixel whatever that pixel is, so a floor drawn near-black gains a smooth wedge and not a lit floor. ONE TORCH, TWO DOORS, FOUR CELLS FROM EACH: the east door opens into a second roofed room and the west door opens to the open sky, so both beams have identical geometry and differ only in what they land on - which is the comparison the report is making. Gravel everywhere, indoors and out, so how much of the ground's own speckle survives inside each beam is measurable and attributable. Arms: vanilla, the shader max as shipped, the surface lift, the shader max again as a same-build control for the run-to-run pixel floor, and the shader-max / surface-lift pair repeated at noon, where the roofed floor is brighter and the lift must therefore be smaller. Every cloud flag is off and stated: half this scene is open ground directly under a measured beam.",
